About 25,000 Chicago 3rd, 6th and 8th graders will be required to attend summer school this year because they performed below the Board of Education's standards on the latest Iowa Tests of Basic Skills, according to data released Thursday by the Chicago Public Schools.
The figure represents the smallest class of students being required to attend the Summer Bridge program since 1997. If the students don't improve over the summer, they risk being flunked in August, school officials said.
"The figures show our students continue to improve," schools chief Paul Vallas said.
The figures include 10,675 3rd graders, the fewest in three years, as well as 6,760 6th graders and 7,637 8th graders. Those numbers were lower than the previous year, even though standards for 6th and 8th graders were raised, Vallas noted.
Meanwhile, school officials released preliminary Iowa test data on the performance of the city's nine charter schools. Overall, Vallas called those scores "disappointing." At least two schools may be at risk of having their charters revoked for poor academic performance after a formal assessment of all the charters is completed in the summer, he added.
"I hoped they would perform better," Vallas said. "Schools that have had three years to improve scores have had plenty of time to make progress."
The results show that Chicago's charter schools continue to struggle with significant obstacles, including attracting some of the system's most impoverished children and pupils who have had difficulty making educational progress at other public schools.
At most of the schools, less than half of the students meet national norms for reading and math.
Of particular concern to the central office are ACT Charter and Chicago International Longwood, each of which posted three consecutive years of declining reading scores on the Iowa test.
At ACT Charter, only 16.8 percent of pupils in the elementary grades can read at grade level, the lowest for any charter school in Chicago.
Sarah Howard, a school director, said her data shows most students improved at least a whole grade level in reading and math during the last year.
"We know there's learning going on," said Howard, whose students come largely from the Garfield Park neighborhood on the West Side. "We are working very hard and feel progress is being made."
Greg Richmond, who directs charters for the Chicago Public Schools, saved his most biting comments for Octavio Paz Charter, even though the school posted improved reading scores compared with a year ago. Its math scores dipped, however, over the same period.
