Her blindness led to her living the life of a recluse, suffering a broken leg and a catastrophic house fire. But after two years of sightlessness, the prize-winning novelist Candia McWilliam can once again see.
McWilliam's sight was restored five weeks ago after pioneering surgery cured the rare disorder, blepharospasm, which forced her eyelids to close permanently over her otherwise healthy eyes. Living without sight, she said, "was frightening". "My life is visual I am not athletic. My gift was to see the world and interpret it."
