His retrospective of the classic British post-World War II films begins March 1 with "Whisky Galore!" and continues with "A Run for Your Money" (March 7), "Kind Hearts and Coronets" (March 22), "The Man in the White Suit" (March 29), "The Lavender Hill Mob (April 5) and "The Ladykillers" (April 12). All six were made between 1949 and 1955 and five featured Alec Guinness, who became an international star as a result.
"Ealing had quite an effect on world cinema during that era," Hoffman said. "Even now, anytime you see a quirky comedy with an ensemble cast, these films probably had an influence — especially in England."
Hoffman picked precisely the opposite narrative tone when he chose film noir as the theme for his debut series at the library in 2009. "Out of the Past," the 1947 classic starring Robert Mitchum, and the first film he programmed for the library, is hardly a laugh riot.
Hoffman is aware comedy is a crowd pleaser, and this is the third time he's returned to the genre including series featuring silent film and comedy teams. Yet the comedies he chooses also serve his primary purpose: to introduce viewers to less-well-known films from the past.
"I always include films I think the audience might not be familiar with," said Hoffman, who also programs the successful Pickwick Theatre Classic Film Series. "Often, they're films I personally enjoy that I want to share. I like seeing people making new discoveries and enjoying them."
