At its Aug. 28 meeting, the Solano County Board of Supervisors approved the placement of five Tuff Sheds next to county-owned property on Brown Street near the William J. Carroll. The sheds are intended to house 10 selected homeless individuals to participate in a county program. While the tiny houses do not have electricity or plumbing, it would have nearby portable bathrooms and bins with trash collectors. Showers would also be provided twice weekly by Opportunity House.
The project was spearheaded by Supervisor Skip Thomson who had seen a similar project in Yuba County. The supervisors voted 4-0 to approve the project, as Chairwoman Erin Hannigan was absent.
However, the project quickly drew backlash with Vacaville residents expressing their concerns at subsequent Board of Supervisors meetings. Among the issues expressed: the lack of electricity and plumbing in the sheds, being too close in proximity to Markham Elementary School and a potential increase in crime.
The protests trickled over to Vacaville City Council meetings, but the council had to repeatedly remind speakers that since the project would be on county-owned land, the city did not have jurisdiction over the property use.
Nonetheless, the council requested at its Nov. 13 meeting the county provide information about the project. Additionally, the council directed staff at its Jan. 22 meeting to bring back a letter of opposition to the proposed location of the project.
A letter to Hannigan written by Mayor Ron Rowlett on behalf of the entire council was included in the staff report for Tuesday��s item. In the letter, Rowlett and the council acknowledged that homelessness was an issue in the city, county and nation as a whole, but felt the proposed location was not one they could support at the moment.
